---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/computer-science/foundational/growth-of-functions');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const growthCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'x', label: 'O(n)', color: '#0e7490' },
    { expr: 'x*log2(x)', label: 'O(n log n)', color: '#1f6f54' },
    { expr: 'x^2', label: 'O(n²)', color: '#b45309' },
    { expr: '2^(x/2)', label: 'O(2ⁿ)', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  x: { min: 1, max: 22, label: 'input size n' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 460, label: 'operations' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 370,
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>The idea</h2>
          <p>
            An algorithm's cost isn't a number — it's a <em>curve</em>. Ask "how long does this program
            take?" and the honest answer is never "one second"; it is "one second <em>for this input, and
            what happens as the input grows?</em>" Two programs can both be correct, both run fine on your
            laptop, and yet one finishes in a blink and the other outlives the universe on a dataset a
            million times larger. Big-O notation names the difference: it describes the <strong>shape</strong>
            of the cost curve as input size <code>n</code> grows, ignoring constant factors and small-input
            noise, because shape is what decides everything at scale. Which programs finish and which spin
            forever, which datasets fit and which don't — that is decided by the curve, and the curves are
            surprisingly few. Four shapes dominate; here they are against input size <code>n</code>:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>operations vs input size — the quadratic and exponential leave everything behind</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="plotter" data-config={JSON.stringify(growthCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>Reading the curve: the common complexity classes</h2>
          <p>
            Work through the shapes with real numbers. <strong>O(1)</strong>, constant: the cost does not
            depend on n at all — looking up an array element by index takes one step whether the array has
            10 slots or 10 million. <strong>O(log n)</strong>, logarithmic: each step throws away half the
            remaining input. Binary search on a million sorted items needs about 20 steps (2²⁰ ≈ 10⁶);
            on a billion items, about 30. Doubling the data adds exactly one step. <strong>O(n)</strong>,
            linear: touch every item once — summing a list, finding a maximum. Double the data, double the
            work. <strong>O(n log n)</strong>: merge sort's signature — log n levels of work, each level
            touching n items; this is the practical floor for comparison-based sorting, and it stays
            near-linear forever. <strong>O(n²)</strong>, quadratic: compare every item with every other —
            bubble sort, checking all pairs. n = 1,000 costs a million steps (fine); n = 100,000 costs ten
            billion (not fine). <strong>O(2ⁿ)</strong>, exponential: try every subset — brute-forcing
            which items to pack in a knapsack. Each extra item <em>doubles</em> the cost; at n = 60 there
            are more steps than grains of sand on Earth. Brute force dies here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Common misconception:</em> Big-O is not a running time and not a prediction in seconds —
            it is a growth <em>rate</em>. "O(n²) takes longer than O(n)" is only guaranteed
            <em>asymptotically</em>: for tiny inputs the "worse" algorithm can win, because Big-O hides
            constant factors. Insertion sort (O(n²)) genuinely beats merge sort on lists of a dozen items —
            which is exactly why real libraries hand small sublists to insertion sort and only deploy the
            asymptotic winner at scale. Zoom into the left edge of the plot above and you can see the
            curves tangle; shape only asserts itself at scale, and that is why engineers profile before
            optimising. (One refinement completes the picture: time is not the only cost with a shape.
            <strong>Space complexity</strong> — how much memory an algorithm needs as n grows — obeys
            the same notation, and the two trade against each other constantly: merge sort needs O(n)
            extra space to gain its O(n log n) speed, while heapsort sorts in place with O(1) extra
            memory; caching effects mean "fewer bytes moved" is often the real reason one algorithm
            beats another, which is why complexity analysis and measurement are companions, not
            rivals.)
          </p>

          <h2>Where curves come from: designing and creating a solution</h2>
          <p>
            Complexity is not an accident; it is designed in. The standard method for building an
            algorithm starts with <strong>abstraction</strong> — discarding details irrelevant to the
            problem (a route-planner keeps road connections and journey times, forgets scenery and
            tarmac) — followed by <strong>decomposition</strong>: break the problem into sub-problems
            until each is directly codeable. Design the algorithm <em>before</em> the code: in structured
            English, in pseudocode, or as a flowchart. Pseudocode is the working language because it
            forces every decision (what loop, what condition, what variables) while staying
            implementation-free. Two design habits matter more than cleverness: choosing the right
            <strong>data structure</strong> (a sorted array enables binary search — O(log n) where a
            linear scan would pay O(n); a set makes membership checks constant-time) and recognising
            nested work. The heuristic that catches most disasters early: <em>count the loops</em>. One
            loop over n items is O(n); a loop inside a loop over the same n is usually O(n²); a loop that
            halves the problem each pass is O(log n). The widget's curves are, to first approximation,
            just loop-nesting made visible.
          </p>

          <h2>Standard methods of solution</h2>
          <p>
            Most algorithms are assembled from a small toolkit of patterns, and examiners (and
            interviewers) expect you to recognise them by name. <strong>Totalling</strong>: initialise an
            accumulator to 0 before a loop, add each value inside it. <strong>Counting</strong>: the same
            skeleton, but add 1 only when a condition holds — count the passing grades, the negative
            temperatures, the vowels. <strong>Maximum/minimum</strong>: set the running best to the first
            value, then replace it whenever a new value beats it. <strong>Linear search</strong>: walk the
            list until you find the target or run out — O(n), works on any order. <strong>Binary
            search</strong>: check the middle item, discard the half that cannot contain the target,
            repeat — O(log n), but demands sorted data (a fact worth pausing on: sorting costs n log n
            once, then every search is logarithmic — which is why databases fight so hard to stay
            indexed). <strong>Sorting</strong>: bubble sort swaps adjacent out-of-order pairs repeatedly —
            O(n²) but simple; merge sort splits, sorts the halves recursively, and merges — O(n log n).
            <strong>Validation</strong> (check input is sensible before accepting: range checks, type
            checks, presence checks) and <strong>verification of totals</strong> (checksums) round out the
            standard patterns. Knowing these cold is what "standard methods of solution" means: you are
            not inventing algorithms, you are recognising which classic the problem secretly is.
          </p>

          <h2>Test data, trace tables and debugging</h2>
          <p>
            A program that works on the happy path is not yet correct. <strong>Test data</strong> comes in
            planned categories. <em>Normal</em> data: values the program should accept and process
            correctly (a valid mark of 65). <em>Boundary</em> (extreme) data: the exact edges of valid
            ranges — for marks valid in 0–100, test 0, 100, and the neighbours −1 and 101; off-by-one
            errors (using <code><</code> where <code><=</code> belonged) are invisible to normal
            tests and fatal at the boundary. <em>Erroneous (abnormal)</em> data: values that should be
            rejected with a sensible message, not a crash or silent nonsense ("twenty", a mark of 999, a
            blank field). The discipline is to design the test table <em>before</em> running anything,
            stating each input and its expected output — otherwise you will unconsciously test what the
            program does well.
          </p>
          <p>
            When it does go wrong, the primary manual tool is the <strong>trace table</strong>: a grid
            with a column per variable, one row per pass through the loop, recording every change of
            value. Tracing swaps in a bubble sort on the list [5, 2, 9] — watching first-pass comparisons
            and swaps unfold row by row — exposes in seconds whether the algorithm ever touches what it
            should. Trace tables catch three failure families: loops that run once too often (the final
            row shows a value creeping past its bound), counters mis-initialised (a total that starts at
            1 instead of 0 poisons every later row), and conditions that never fire (a column that stays
            at its initial value for the whole run). Modern practice adds debuggers, print statements and
            automated test suites, but they all implement the same idea: slow the program down until the
            wrong <em>step</em>, not just the wrong answer, is visible.
          </p>

          <h2>Writing and amending algorithms</h2>
          <p>
            The final skill is production: translating a design into clean pseudocode and then
            <em>amending</em> it safely — because requirements change. The rules of good algorithmic
            writing are unfashionable but real: meaningful identifier names (<code>totalHours</code>,
            not <code>t</code>), one entry and one exit point per structure, no duplicated logic (extract
            it into a function), and initialisation immediately before use. Amendments then become
            local: changing a validation range, adding a counting branch, swapping a linear search for a
            binary one — each is a one-line edit in a well-named algorithm and a debugging nightmare in a
            tangled one. When you amend, re-run the trace table on the new logic and re-check the
            boundaries you did not touch; the classic amendment bug is fixing one boundary and breaking
            its twin. And after every change, re-ask the complexity question: did this edit change the
            <em>shape</em>? A "small fix" that nests one loop inside another has moved the program from
            the teal curve to the orange one, and no amount of hardware heroics will fully buy that back.
          </p>

          <h2>One task, three algorithms: a worked comparison</h2>
          <p>
            Concrete numbers make the curves land. Task: find a single name in a sorted list of
            1,000,000 entries. <em>Linear search</em> (O(n)): on average half the list — 500,000
            comparisons; at 100 ns per comparison, 50 milliseconds — imperceptible once. <em>Binary
            search</em> (O(log n)): about 20 comparisons — 2 microseconds, 25,000× faster.
            Now scale to a billion entries: linear becomes 50 seconds per lookup (per lookup!),
            binary is still ~30 comparisons. The <em>sort</em> that enables binary search is a
            one-time O(n log n) cost — about 20 billion comparisons for the billion-entry list, a
            few minutes once — after which every one of the millions of daily lookups is logarithmic.
            Amortise and the trade is a landslide. Now the other direction: a bubble sort on that
            same billion entries at n² = 10¹⁸ operations is, at 100 ns each, roughly <em>three
            thousand years</em>. Same machine, same data, same problem — the only variable was the
            shape of the algorithm's cost curve. This is why "growth of functions" is not an abstract
            maths detour but the difference between a feature that ships and one that never finishes
            its first run.
          </p>

          <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Hardware can't save you.</strong> A 1,000× faster chip buys a quadratic algorithm
            roughly 10√1000 ≈ 31.6 times the input (not 1,000), a linear one the full 1,000, and an
            exponential one less than 10 extra items (log₂1000 ≈ 10). A better algorithm buys millions.
            This asymmetry is why complexity is called the one free lunch in computer science.
            <strong>Big-O is an upper bound</strong>, stated with its own algebra: constants drop
            (O(3n² + 500n) = O(n²)), products add in sequence (an O(n) pass followed by an O(n log n)
            sort is O(n log n)), and nested loops multiply. <strong>Ω (omega)</strong> and
            <strong>Θ (theta)</strong> refine the story — lower bound and tight bound — and the deepest
            fact in the field lives there: comparison-based sorting provably cannot beat n log n, so
            merge sort isn't just good, it is optimal-in-class. Harder still are the
            <strong>NP-hard</strong> problems — travelling salesman, knapsack at scale, timetabling —
            where every known algorithm is exponential and thousands of researchers suspect (the
            unresolved P vs NP question, the most famous open problem in computer science) that no fast
            algorithm exists at all. Practice there means heuristics: approximate answers, fast, rather
            than exact answers, never. The plot above is, in the end, a map of the computational
            universe: the flatlands where everything is easy, the hills where careful design is needed,
            and the walls beyond which computation itself runs out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, Vol. 3 (sorting and searching); Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest & Stein, Introduction to Algorithms (4th ed.); Cambridge International AS & A Level Computer Science 9618 syllabus (algorithm design and problem-solving).</p>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
